's  absolutely central to the story and to  the conflict that John Smith goes  through and it makes the doctor coming  back have that tinge of melancholy and  you feel a little bit bad about it and  Jessica Hines as Joan Redford is just  wonderful in her chemistry with David  Tennant is fantastic and now even though  I said when you lose John Smith you feel  a little bit back because it does feel  like a death when the doctor comes back  and you realize he's back that is one of  the wonderful all-time triumphant  moments of this show but then at the  same time do almost immediately undercut  it with the way that he punishes the  family of blood for their pursuit of him  it's a dark place and you know it got  paid off further because it got explored  more in some of the later episodes so  moving on to number two  the impossible planet and the Satan  pitch now this one honestly is slightly  a nostalgic choice because the Satan pit  was the first episode of Doctor Who I  ever paid attention to I'm not gonna say  it's the first one I ever saw but the  first one I ever stopped what I was  doing and went huh  this is a high level high stakes Horror  Story which you don't see a lot of  generally for horror stories to work  they need to be very small very intimate  they need to feel like it's happening  sort of right over your shoulder dealing  with huge or implications with things  like the devil generally these horror  stories tend to fall apart cuz they get  a little bit too grand and a little bit  too fancy this one still works I think  as a horror story and it's got that same  quality that I mentioned in midnight  which is that the supporting cast feels  very genuine I was actually really  surprised when I went back and caught up  on this that none of those characters  had been in the series up to that point  because they felt really lived in and  their interaction was great it felt like  the kind of chemistry that characters  would have who've been around each other  for a long time and the music is really  wonderful the music in this episode in  the impossible planet especially is  unlike almost anything else that  Marigold has ever composed for the show  it's just so  when Kali and so lonely it sounds almost  more like music from Firefly you know  the space equivalent of the desert feel  to it that just helps the atmosphere all  the more and the way this thing built I  mean great pacing has been a hallmark of  this top 10 list and again wonderful  pacing to a really excellent climax and  it's hard to pay off a story of where  you confront the devil and have it work  properly and I will grant that once he  gives his hand on the tars it basically  just becomes a magic button that allows  him to save everybody maybe that's the  one reason it does fall short of my top  choice which does bring me to my number  one pick Cylons in the library and the  forest of the Dead now if you have been  following my rankings on Doctor Who this  one should not come as a huge surprise  because first of all I love Donna she is  my favorite companion bonus we've also  got the introduction of dr. River sonic  who I like again I know not everybody  does but I liked River Song and I loved  her introduction again I mentioned  already  I like the timey wimey stories I like  when things happen to the doctor  somewhat out of order and for him to  meet someone who is going to end up  being so important and so vital to his  life and the first time he meets her is  the time that she dies I thought going  back and watching this episode now I do  get choked up not necessarily by the  death itself but I feel her pain and  looking at him and him not recognizing  her I mean it was  I loved this episode when it first air  but it's gotten better as her story has  panned out more since then it's added  extra weight to the story that I already  loved I loved the Vashta Nerada the the  shadows that eat you what a wonderful  concept and once again a supporting cast  with great chemistry that they feel  lived in and they the interactions feel  very genuine and these characters feel  fleshed I don't even though we don't see  a ton of the movie we haven't seen them  before  I love dr. moon I love Colin samithy so  I think he's a wonderful and  underappreciated actor this is just a  wonderful episode I think it's a  fantastic self-contained story  it's got my favorite campaign it's got  the introduction of one of my other  favorite companions great monster  great setting great ideas it's my  personal favorite David Tennant 10th  doctor story but as I said I would not  be shocked at all if other people's top  10 rankings are completely different  than mine in fact I'm sure some of you  have already started writing your angry  comments about why I'm an Indian and  what your ranking is so whatever is your  personal favorite David Tennant doctor  whose story drop it on down in the  comments or give me a top 10 list for a  David Tennant if you feel up for that  whatever it is drop it down in the  comments and let's talk about it until  next time this council is adjourned
 

